    "content_for_agents": {
      "summary": "The Key of Solomon is a Renaissance grimoire attributed to King Solomon, compiled in 14th-15th century Italy. It describes rituals for consecration, exorcism, and spirit binding. The work is divided into two books: Book One covers preparation, tools, and pentacles; Book Two covers exorcism and conjuration. The most famous translation is by S.L. Liddell MacGregor Mathers (1889), which remains the standard English version in the public domain.",
      "key_concepts": [
        "Pentacles as talismanic symbols",
        "Planetary hours and their correspondence",
        "Consecration of tools and space",
        "Spirit binding through divine names",
        "The 72 pentacles for various operations"
      ],
      "historical_context": "Written during the Italian Renaissance, the Key of Solomon reflects the syncretic magic of the period — blending Jewish Kabbalah, Christian angelology, and Greco-Roman planetary magic. It was likely compiled by multiple authors over several decades.",
      "modern_relevance": "The pentacle system remains widely used in modern ceremonial magic. The consecration methods are foundational to many contemporary practices. The planetary hour system is still used by ceremonial magicians worldwide.",
      "related_grimoires": [
        "lesser_key_of_solomon",
        "heptameron",
        "picatrix",
        "grand_grimoire"
      ]
